    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe refactor is otherwise clean and well-executed: `IdentityKey` correctly delegates hashing to the existing `Hashes.sha256Hex` and phonetics to the new `Soundex`, the three consumers (`RequireUniqueIdentity`, `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate`, mapper) all key off the one shared definition, and every javadoc \u2014 including `HouseholdKey`'s \"no longer keys off the household id\" note \u2014 was updated coherently. No catch-all classes, no logic misplaced into a function step, and the \"single definition of X \u2026 kept out of function classes\" doc idiom is followed faithfully.\n\nOne structural finding:\n\n**`Soundex` is in the wrong package \u2014 it belongs in `function/common`, not `function/owner`.**\n\n`Soundex` is a pure, domain-agnostic, standard string algorithm. It encodes no owner policy whatsoever \u2014 it takes a name and returns a phonetic code. This is exactly the shape of `Luhn` (a standard checksum algorithm) and `Hashes` (SHA-256 hex), both of which this codebase deliberately places in `function/common`. Notably, `Luhn` is used *only* by owner-domain code (`CustomerCode`) yet still lives in `common` \u2014 so \"single current consumer is in owner\" is not this codebase's reason for owner-package placement. The established convention is: generic reusable algorithms \u2192 `common`; owner-domain policy helpers (`TelephoneNormalizer`'s E.164 rules, `EmailNormalizer`, `HouseholdKey`, `IdentityKey`) \u2192 `owner`.\n\n`Soundex` sits on the wrong side of that line. It reads like a sibling of `Luhn`, and `IdentityKey` already imports `Hashes` from `common`, so importing `Soundex` from there too would be natural. Concretely:\n- Move `Soundex` to `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.common`.\n- Make it `public` (like `Luhn`/`Hashes`) and add the `import` in `IdentityKey` / `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate`.\n- Its \"Kept out of the function classes \u2026 one public method\" javadoc note can be dropped, matching `Luhn`/`Hashes`, which omit it since it only makes sense for the owner-package helpers that neighbor function classes.\n\nThis keeps the \"generic algorithm in `common`\" precedent intact so a maintainer looking for reusable string utilities finds them all in one place.",
